  "intro": "A Taylor series expands $f$ about $x=a$: $f(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n$. A Maclaurin series is the special case $a=0$. Key Maclaurin series to know: $e^x=\\sum\\frac{x^n}{n!}$, $\\sin x=\\sum\\frac{(-1)^n x^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}$, $\\cos x=\\sum\\frac{(-1)^n x^{2n}}{(2n)!}$, and $\\frac{1}{1-x}=\\sum x^n$ for $|x|<1$. Taylor polynomials (partial sums) approximate $f$ near $a$.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Find the Maclaurin series for $e^x$ from the definition.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "All derivatives of $e^x$ are $e^x$, and at $a=0$ each equals $1$.",
          "So $\\frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}=\\frac{1}{n!}$.",
          "The series is $\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\frac{x^n}{n!}=1+x+\\frac{x^2}{2}+\\frac{x^3}{6}+\\cdots$."
        ],
        "answer": "$e^x=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\dfrac{x^n}{n!}$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Write the Maclaurin series for $\\cos x$ and its first three nonzero terms.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$\\cos x=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\frac{(-1)^n x^{2n}}{(2n)!}$.",
          "Write out $n=0,1,2$: $1-\\frac{x^2}{2!}+\\frac{x^4}{4!}$.",
          "So the first three nonzero terms are $1-\\frac{x^2}{2}+\\frac{x^4}{24}$."
        ],
        "answer": "$1-\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{x^4}{24}-\\cdots$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Find the Taylor polynomial of degree $2$ for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $a=4$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "$f(4)=2$; $f'(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}$ gives $f'(4)=\\frac{1}{4}$.",
          "$f''(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}x^{-3/2}$ gives $f''(4)=-\\frac{1}{32}$, so $\\frac{f''(4)}{2!}=-\\frac{1}{64}$.",
          "$T_2(x)=2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{64}(x-4)^2$."
        ],
        "answer": "$T_2(x)=2+\\dfrac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\dfrac{1}{64}(x-4)^2$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
